{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He played a significant role in the history of the Crusades and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem was a sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem and King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \n*Reign* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli reigned for more than thirty years and his reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim states of Syria. He also had to deal with the internal conflicts of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and he often had disagreements with him over issues such as territory and power. He was also a regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for a short period of time after the death of King Amalric I in 1174. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and had significant consequences for the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n*Capture and Imprisonment* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was captured by the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din in 1164 and was imprisoned for several years. He was eventually released and returned to his county where he continued to play a significant role in the politics of the region. \n*Conflict with Saladin* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli also had conflicts with the famous Muslim leader Saladin who was the Sultan of Egypt and Syria. He fought against Saladin in several battles and was able to defend his county against the Muslim forces.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.7751135399